      "rev_n_1_a06799_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_a06799_1",
        "node_id": "n_1_a06799",
        "thought_text": "The current approach counts paths twice for each unique path (once from each end), leading to overcounting. To fix this, we should only count paths from one direction and avoid double-counting.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_1_a06799_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_41778f7eae",
        "created_at": 1794533090.7741964
      },
      "rev_n_2_ab3362_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_ab3362_1",
        "node_id": "n_2_ab3362",
        "thought_text": "I apologize for the oversight. The error in the previous implementation was due to counting paths twice. Let's modify the approach to correctly count only unique paths of length 2. We should consider each path only once, from the lower-numbered junction to the higher-numbered junction. This will ensure we don't double-count paths and correctly handle cases where a path exists in both directions.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_2_ab3362_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_f72a49f5b9",
        "created_at": 1794533129.377546
      },
      "rev_n_3_11e23f_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_11e23f_1",
        "node_id": "n_3_11e23f",
        "thought_text": "The current approach counts paths twice for each node, leading to overcounting. Instead, we should count paths from each node to its neighbors and sum these counts. This ensures we don't double-count paths and correctly identify optimal paths of length 2.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_3_11e23f_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_46b94cbf51",
        "created_at": 1794533164.450647
